Separate disturbance rejection from sensor bias and identifiable quantities (@engineering-feedback-observation: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Find-attainable-equilibria-when-the-actuator-saturates.lingua#r_007033EFE8056B437FB9B91733") { r_0089FCA742A41805791FE10944
The equilibrium true error is e*=r−θ*=(Gr−u₀−d+Kb)/(G+K). With r fixed, sensitivities are ∂θ*/∂d=1/(G+K) and ∂θ*/∂b=−K/(G+K). Larger K attenuates the effect of a constant plant disturbance but transfers more of the sensor offset into the true state. If u₀=Gr, d=0 and b≠0, increasing gain drives θ* toward r−b, not toward r. The observation y*=θ*+b can approach r while the useful state retains error.

Distinguish a deterministic constant bias from random noise. The present derivation does not supply a noise distribution, a frequency response or a stochastic stability claim. Time-varying measurement error and finite sensor dynamics need explicit models. Use [Review fitting evidence before extending scientific claims](../../inquiry/core/Review fitting evidence before extending scientific claims.lingua#r_008E6FD7D2B90DB8ED1CE9927E) and the existing uncertainty preparation to keep parameter uncertainty, repeatability and model discrepancy separate. A fitted curve does not establish the correct measurement boundary.

Even noiseless measurement histories can fail to separate parameters. Substitute θ=y−b into the differential equation, assuming constant b. Then Cy′=u₀+Kr+d+Gb−(G+K)y. With known C,G,K,u₀,r, only the combination d+Gb enters this observation equation. For any δ, changing b to b+δ and d to d−Gδ while changing the initial true state to θ_i−δ leaves the entire y(t) history unchanged. The true temperatures differ. This is a structural ambiguity proved by substitution, not a consequence of too few digits or an unlucky sample.

Changing r or the nominal gain alone does not remove this particular ambiguity if the same constant d,b and observation boundary remain: the same combination d+Gb survives. An independently referenced temperature measurement can constrain b; a separately characterized disturbance can constrain d. Changing a known physical G while keeping the offset and disturbance unchanged could also separate their contributions, but those invariance premises need evidence. Recording more points on the same ambiguous trajectory is insufficient.

Parameter scale creates other ambiguities. When C,G,K and all powers are scaled together, the state equation is unchanged; known controller calibration or independent power/capacity measurements may break that ambiguity. Reuse [Choose observations that separate source and connection explanations](Electrical-observation-loading-and-identifiability.lingua#r_00EEBD1BA46C0A10C2842CDFF9) and [Compare thermal alternatives and the evidence needed to choose](Compare-thermal-alternatives-and-the-evidence-needed-to-choose.lingua#r_00B7A969633B37AA7737D882CB) for the general distinction between a fitted response and separately identifiable physical components.

**Work:** choose two distinct (d,b,θ_i) triples with the same observed trajectory, derive their true tracking errors and identify an added measurement that distinguishes them. Have a critic identify a hidden assumption in that added measurement. State which quantities a proposed acceptance record actually establishes and which remain conditional.

**Continue:** [Find attainable equilibria when the actuator saturates](Find-attainable-equilibria-when-the-actuator-saturates.lingua#r_007033EFE8056B437FB9B91733).

